### Dataset qualifications


**ACFE:** The study contains 2,402 usable cases across 143 countries/territories. Respondents described their largest completed occupational-fraud investigation within January 2024–September 2025. Question-level denominators vary. Industry case counts therefore do not measure fraud frequency in industry populations; sector mean losses were not established. ([acfe.jp](https://www.acfe.jp/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/RTTN_2026-Digital.pdf))

**DBIR:** This is a contributed, non-random case corpus. Its methodology explicitly acknowledges disclosure and sampling bias, including possible ransomware overrepresentation. The edition’s main data relate to 2025; an exact common observation window for every industry-table entry was not established. ([verizon.com](https://www.verizon.com/business/resources/T343/reports/2026-dbir-data-breach-investigations-report.pdf))

**UK cloud indicator:** The 2026 survey received 196 responses, with 105 completing the full questionnaire. Most detailed results use full-survey, valid question-level responses. The cloud indicator concerns artificial-intelligence provision, not all outsourced technology. ([bankofengland.co.uk](https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/report/2026/artificial-intelligence-in-uk-financial-services-2026))



**Loss boundary:** Occupational fraud, telecom revenue fraud, customer scam losses and breach-response costs are different outcomes. Combining them would obscure who loses money, what caused the loss and which controls are relevant.


