## F. Preliminary telecommunications statement


**Inherent cyber / ICT exposure - Medium confidence:** Telecommunications has documented exposure through network-device management and trusted interconnections, alongside continuing technology migration and shared infrastructure arrangements. The evidence establishes these mechanisms, but not a quantified sector-wide likelihood or a ranking against the four peers.

**Observed maturity - Medium confidence:** The retrieved EU and UK evidence supports an established and improving security baseline, with remaining weaknesses in supplier assurance, security testing and access management. It does not support describing the whole sector as uniformly immature.

**Preparedness - Medium confidence for identified gaps; Low for global generalisation:** Recovery testing is not uniformly routine in the combined telecom/cloud survey group. Historical operator outages also demonstrate common-mode failure and emergency-service continuity weaknesses. Neither establishes a global telecom recovery-time distribution.

**Overall:** Inherent technological exposure and observed control effectiveness should remain separate conclusions. Evidence of investment or supervisory maturity does not eliminate the documented attack pathways; evidence of an intrusion or outage does not establish sector-wide control failure.


## G. Subsector variation, limitations and missing indicators

### Subsector boundaries



| Subsector | Supported distinction | Remaining gap |
|---|---|---|
| MNOs | Deployment and signalling-access evidence applies most directly here | No harmonised prevalence of network functions virtualisation, Open Radio Access Network deployment, second-/third-generation legacy exposure or signalling-control effectiveness |
| Mobile virtual network operators (MVNOs) | No MVNO-specific metric was established | Separate full/light operating models, host-network dependencies and retained provisioning responsibilities before comparison |
| Fixed-line and broadband | Company-level fibre migration and common-core failure evidence exists | No comparable population measure of exposed customer equipment, legacy platforms or restoration performance |
| Wholesale subsea cable, dark fibre and landing stations | Separate physical-route, landing-site and active-equipment questions are required | No route-diversity, repair-capacity, supplier concentration or cyber-control denominator established |
| Tower companies | Shared ownership is documented, but should not be equated with operating the mobile core | No tower-specific security maturity, power-autonomy or outage distribution established |
| Managed network, cloud and Unified Communications as a Service (UCaaS) | Must remain separate from network operators | The combined telecom/cloud testing statistic cannot establish UCaaS or managed-network preparedness |



### Geographic limits


- **EU:** The strongest cross-sector maturity evidence is available here, but it is an ecosystem assessment rather than a representative audit of every operator.
- **UK:** Supervisory and incident evidence is more specific, but its reporting periods and provider coverage are not interchangeable with the EU series.
- **US:** The retrieved government advisory establishes attack techniques and affected provider types, not a sector-wide maturity or remediation-completion rate.
- **Worldwide:** GSA deployment counts and multinational case studies provide breadth, but not a harmonised global security denominator.


### Evidence gaps and conflicts


- **CFCA:** The publisher identifies a *Global Fraud Loss Survey 2025*, with a publication announcement dated 10 December 2025. Its full methodology and tables were not retrieved, so no loss estimate is admitted here as a benchmark. ([cfca.org](https://cfca.org/?utm_source=openai))
- **IBM:** The publisher’s 2026 breach-cost landing page was retrieved, but not an inspectable industry table and methodology sufficient for this comparison. Consequently, no secondary breach-cost figure is inserted into peer columns. ([ibm.com](https://www.ibm.com/reports/data-breach))
- **ENISA incident-report consistency:** The executive summary and detailed section disagree on participating-country coverage. The human-error chart rounds differently from the narrative, and third-party-failure counts/percentages vary within the report. Those percentages are not used as comparative indicators. ([enisa.europa.eu](https://www.enisa.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2025-07/ENISA_Telecom_Security_Incidents_2024_en.pdf))



Additional missing indicators include:

- Exposure and control effectiveness for Signalling System No. 7 (SS7), Diameter and GPRS Tunnelling Protocol (GTP).
- Lawful-interception access, customer portals/application programming interfaces and subscriber identity module (SIM) provisioning.
- Toll fraud, international revenue share fraud, SIM swap and subscription-fraud distributions, including who bears the loss.
- Remote/vendor-access prevalence; core, radio-network, cloud and signalling-hub concentration.
- Operations/business support systems automation; IT/operational technology convergence measured consistently across peers.
- Supplier substitutability, customer switching constraints, route diversity and power/cloud dependency.
- Sector-normalised subsidiary counts, network-sharing arrangements, agent networks, acquisition integration and governance enforcement.
- Telecom-only dwell time, eviction difficulty and tested restoration performance.

**Bias controls:** Listed-company disclosures cannot represent private operators automatically. Mandatory outage reports cannot be merged with voluntary breach investigations. More reports may reflect reporting scope or detection rather than more underlying events. No count in this assessment has been converted into a probability.


